            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

AIR SEALING



Overall, your house is moderately leaky from an air leakage perspective, and I have identified a few locations that can be addressed:



** The largest source is the open combustion air pipe (8" in diameter) located behind the coat rack in your mechanical room - this pipe is likely a significant cause of this room being cold. With a furnace upgrade to a new condensing furnace (natural gas) it would eliminate the combustion air pipe.



** Another large source of air leakage identified is coming from the utility crawl space under the main floor of your home. When completing the air leakage test, I noticed any penetration through the floor had air flow associated with it. When I investigated further, in the utility crawl space area, I found a few areas of suspected leakage: 



A potential access point to the crawl space at the back of the house. If this is not a necessary opening, this can be sealed up. In the very least it can be made to be a tighter fit.



The uninsulated header spaces, especially along the front and back walls. These can be insulated with spray foam which will provide insulation value, and an air seal.



Any pipe, wiring, or hose penetration through the crawlspace wall. These can be filled with spray foam or caulked as needed.



** Around the attic hatch is a small source of air leakage. This can be sealed with removable caulking such as "Draft Attack".



** The bathroom exhaust fan was a bit leaky when the home was depressurized. Bathroom fans can be replaced with energy star rated fans with butterfly style dampers, that seal better than traditional exhaust fan dampers.



Savings: For modelling purposes, I estimated a modest 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss, but this is quite conservative, and if you were to tackle all the issues mentioned above, it would result in a much larger reduction. A 10% reduction in air leakage is estimated to save you $130 per year and decrease your annual energy usage by 3.0 GJ.



There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $550 and $1000 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake.



An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

MAIN WALLS



The main exterior walls of your home, for the purposes of our modelling, appear to be 2x6 construction with batt insulation (R20), plus an additional 1.5 inches of semi-rigid fibreglass batt insulation on the outside (R7).



I have added an insulation upgrade recommendation only to provide you with an idea of what your savings would be if your walls had a higher insulation value, similar to what would be seen on a newer home, and is not meant to be something that is highly recommended. I have modelled adding 2 additional inches (R10) of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation to every exterior surface, but this would only be something to consider if you do ever decide to complete a siding upgrade.



Savings: By adding 2 inches (R10) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls, you would save approximately $380 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 8.3 GJ, and qualify for up to a $1,500 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ÂinsulationÂ rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $5,000 is available for adding insulation to exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home, excluding foundation walls, must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant.</mainWalls>
                <doors>

DOOR UPGRADE



The front door of your home is a steel door with polystyrene insulation and I consider it to be in good condition. Weather stripping was noted to be in reasonable shape, but if you notice it is drafty in the winter then weather stripping could be replaced. The savings on installing new doors will be minimal and there are currenlty no rebates available from the GNWT for door replacements.



GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $125 per hinged door system is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. Replacing old, damaged or leaky hinged doors with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy and improve comfort. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.

</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Natural Gas Instantaneous Upgrade from Oil



You currently have a 120 litre oil fired hot water tank with an energy factor of 0.54. You may want to consider replacing your electric existing water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous, condensing, natural gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.95 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.95, could potentially save $420 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 6.4 GJ,  as well as qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------





</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

FURNACE UPGRADE



One of the largest sources of cold air and discomfort found in your home was the combustion air supply pipe that is open, and constantly dumping cold, outside air into your mechanical room during the heating season. This is a typical installation style for older furnaces, but by upgrading to a direct vent furnace the combustion air never interacts with air in the home. It eliminates that cold air source.



NATURAL GAS FURNACE



Your house is currently heated with a newer 87% efficient oil furnace, that is not in need of a replacement. However, I have modeled the following furnace upgrade option for you as you are on the piped natural gas netork in Inuvik:



An ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing natural gas furnace with a minimum efficiency of 97% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



- This upgrade is currently eligible for a $1,500 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). Full program details are outlined on our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program.



This upgrade will eliminate the need for the current chimney and the open combustion air supply pipe in the mechanical room. 



Savings:



By replacing your current propane furnace with a 97% AFUE condensing natural gas furnace that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $1400 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 19 GJ, and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP).



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



GREENER HOMES GRANT



Under the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of is available for replacing an oil- or gas-fired furnace or boiler with an ENERGY STAR certified natural gas- or propane-fired furnace or boiler. To determine eligibility, confirm that the unit is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.



</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

ADDITIONAL COMMENTS



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



NOTE: You have not yet applied to Greenerr Homes so the information in this report is for information purposes only and not applicable unless you apply for and are approved for participation in the Greener Homes Program by Natural Resources Canada (NRCan)



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:



Â In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

Â If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

Â Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

Â All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

Â It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

Â All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.



Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Inuvik, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Pass



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (your kitchen range hood does not vent outside, therefore the bathroom fan and clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 1.9 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace and your domestic hot water tank vent up a chimney and are susceptible to back drafting. Your wood stove could also experience back drafting when you open the door to add wood, during a burn.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

       

        - one bathroom fan

        - a clothes dryer

      

You may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fan because they seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------





Fuel Costs Â Inuvik



The following December, 2021 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Inuvik:



Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.73 per kWh

Oil - $1.73 per liter 

Natural Gas - $37.21 per GJ delivered

Wood - $341.25 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $14.70 per bag, $682.50 per tonne bulk delivered



